---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import Nav from '../../components/sections/Nav.astro'
import Footer from '../../components/sections/Footer.astro'

import BlogArt from '../../components/blog/BlogArt.astro'
import Byline from '../../components/blog/Byline.astro'
import SeriesNav from '../../components/blog/SeriesNav.astro'
import { postBySlug, formatPostDate, postSocialImage } from '../../data/blog'

const post = postBySlug('an-internet-we-can-change')!
---

<Base
  title={`${post.title} — xNet`}
  description={post.description}
  image={postSocialImage(post).src}
  imageAlt={postSocialImage(post).alt}
>
  <Nav />
  <main id="main-content" class="pb-20 sm:pb-28">
    <header class="relative overflow-hidden border-b border-border bg-gray-950">
      <BlogArt slug={post.slug} eager class="absolute inset-0 h-full w-full object-cover" />
      <div
        class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-b from-black/5 via-black/45 to-black/95"
        aria-hidden="true"
      >
      </div>
      <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 pb-12 pt-52 sm:pb-14 sm:pt-64">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3 gap-y-2 text-xs text-white">
          {
            post.draft ? (
              <span>Local preview</span>
            ) : (
              <time datetime={post.pubDate}>{formatPostDate(post.pubDate)}</time>
            )
          }
          <span aria-hidden="true">·</span>
          <span>{post.readingMinutes} minute read</span>
          <span aria-hidden="true">·</span>
          <span>Personal · Community</span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="mt-4 text-4xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-tight text-white sm:text-5xl">
          {post.title}
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-5 max-w-xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-100">
          {post.description}
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 pb-8 pt-10 sm:pt-12">
      <Byline post={post} />
    </div>

    <article
      aria-label={post.title}
      class="prose prose-lg mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 dark:prose-invert prose-headings:tracking-tight prose-a:decoration-gray-400 prose-a:underline-offset-4"
    >
      <p>
        Ever since I got into programming, I've wanted something like a GitHub for everyone: a place
        where you could show what you're working on, invite people to help, and contribute something
        back when you improve someone else's project.
      </p>

      <p>
        What I love about code is how close the blueprint is to the thing itself. You can read the
        instructions for a piece of software, change them, and run what you've changed. Someone
        else's work can become the starting point for yours. That makes a certain kind of
        collaboration feel natural, and I've spent decades wondering how to bring more of it into
        the rest of life.
      </p>

      <p>
        My first startup crowdfunded dinner parties. It was a wildly impractical business idea. But
        we wanted to have more dinner parties with our friends, and I was interested in whether
        technology could help us make that happen.
      </p>

      <p>
        Later, I got into coworking. We took restaurants that were only open for dinner and opened
        them during the day as places to work. As we added locations across New York and San
        Francisco, I had more places to go and more people to meet.
      </p>

      <p class="office-line">My product was my office. I got to work out of it every day.</p>

      <p>
        That was a lovely way to build a product. The technology made it easier to get into spaces
        where people could spend time together, and I got to be one of those people. Improving the
        product meant improving a part of the world I actually lived in.
      </p>

      <p>
        Then COVID happened. It was a hard time to be in coworking. I found myself thinking about
        all the work behind the experience: the project management, the integrations, the
        information spread across tools that I'd had to wrangle as a CTO. Making those things work
        together had taken so much effort. I wanted a better way to keep and use the data underneath
        them.
      </p>

      <p>
        That took me into CRDTs: data structures that let people make changes independently and
        combine them according to shared rules. At Recurse Center, I studied them, built my own, and
        made collaborative document editors and to-do lists. I got excited about what could happen
        when several people had their own copy of something and could keep working on it together.
      </p>

      <p>
        Behind all this, I still wanted the dinner parties and the shared spaces. The data work was
        another way into the same question: how do we make it easier to organise the lives we want
        with the people around us?
      </p>

      <p>
        You can see that question in how difficult it can be to connect things we already know. A
        conversation starts in a DM, becomes an email, turns into a task, and eventually lands in a
        project tool. An idea from a social post might have started all of it. Each piece makes
        sense to the people involved, but keeping the connections intact becomes another job.
      </p>

      <p>
        Agents have made some of this easier for me. As an engineer, I can connect one to GitHub,
        Linear, or Gmail and have it work across them. Yet I still want the connections and the
        understanding we build to be something we can keep, inspect, and use elsewhere. I don't want
        my ability to make sense of my own life to depend on the particular agent I happened to
        assemble it with.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is where xNet comes from. I've written about <a href="/blog/data-should-work-like-soil"
          >data as soil</a
        > and <a href="/blog/clutch-power">interfaces that fit together like bricks</a>. The
        underlying hope is that information can support more than the first application built around
        it. Your plans, conversations, and work should remain useful as you find new ways to look at
        them and new people to work with.
      </p>

      <p>
        For a long time, I concentrated on that foundation. You would still need to be an engineer
        to build much on top of it. Now coding agents are making it possible to start with an idea,
        describe what you want, and get something you can try. The distance has become shorter,
        though there's still plenty of work between generated code and a tool you can trust.
      </p>

      <p>
        That changes what I want to build next. I'd like xNet to be a place where friends can talk,
        make things, and change the tools they use together. The current desktop prototype starts
        with chat and lets you bring a local coding agent into the process. We're working on
        extensions whose versions and development conversations stay connected, so the next change
        has somewhere to begin.
      </p>

      <p>
        Imagine bringing this back to a group that wants to host more dinners. Someone shares an
        idea in the chat, and a friend brings in a planning tool another group made. Together they
        try it, notice what's missing, and ask an agent to help change it. One person wants a
        simpler way to volunteer to cook. Another needs to use the whole thing with a keyboard.
        Someone else wants to see what changed before the group adopts it.
      </p>

      <p>
        As the tool improves, they can share that version with the group they borrowed it from. The
        useful pattern from open source is there: show your work, learn from what already exists,
        and make it possible for someone else to build on what you learned.
      </p>

      <p>
        The dinner itself still needs people to shop, cook, and clean up. Sharing a plan doesn't
        make that labour disappear. What I want is for the tooling to be something the people doing
        the work can shape, rather than another fixed set of rules they have to work around.
      </p>

      <p>
        For this to be more than a collection of disconnected mini-apps, they need some common
        foundations. A task needs a stable identity so two tools can refer to the same task. They
        need an agreed way to describe what it means, and rules about who can read or change it.
        People need to save work on their own devices, exchange changes, and understand which
        version they're looking at.
      </p>

      <p>
        Those are the kinds of primitives I want to last. They should let a community change its
        calendar or planning board without rebuilding its whole digital life. Open formats and
        documented interfaces would also let other people build tools that I never thought to make.
        A group could share its planning software while keeping its guest list and private
        conversations to itself.
      </p>

      <p>
        I want enough shared structure for things to fit together, with room for people to decide
        what they want to build. Their communities will change. The architecture needs to leave
        space for that.
      </p>

      <p>
        A large part of this is consent. Our software is already intimate. It holds our private
        conversations, our unfinished plans, and things about us that we might choose to share with
        one person and keep from another. Connecting more of that information makes the quality of
        those choices matter even more.
      </p>

      <p>
        The idea of enthusiastic consent, as people discuss it in intimate spaces and sacred
        sexuality, feels useful here. I want to bring that attention to willingness and boundaries
        into the way we relate to software. An agreement should be something we understand and can
        revisit as the relationship changes.
      </p>

      <p>
        With the dinner planner, that could mean saying: you can use this planning conversation and
        the recipes I've chosen to share. My other messages stay private. Let me review what you're
        going to send to the group. If I like how that works, perhaps I'll allow more next time.
      </p>

      <p>
        An agent could help us work out those boundaries. It could explain what a tool wants to do,
        offer a narrower choice, and turn our preferences into rules we can inspect. Later, I could
        say that a reminder was helpful but a suggestion felt intrusive, and change how the tool
        works with me.
      </p>

      <p>
        The software has to enforce what we agree to. A reassuring conversation alone can't provide
        that. There should be visible controls for people who prefer them, a way to say no without
        repeated pressure, and a way to stop future access. It also needs to be honest that stopping
        access cannot recall information already shared.
      </p>

      <p>
        The group matters here too. My wish to connect everything doesn't give me permission to open
        up someone else's private life. Community software needs room for different boundaries,
        including people who want to take part without using an agent at all.
      </p>

      <p>
        That is one reason I care about making this approachable. The person who knows what their
        community needs may have no interest in programming. They should still be able to suggest a
        change, try a version, or help someone understand why a feature isn't working for them. Ink
        &amp; Switch's work on <a href="https://www.inkandswitch.com/essay/malleable-software/"
          >malleable software</a
        > explores this gradual path from using software to changing it, with help from other people.
      </p>

      <p>
        I'd like that help to be part of the experience. A friend who understands the code might
        check a change. Someone who uses a screen reader might notice a problem the rest of the
        group missed. Taking part should not require everyone to have the same skills, a paid agent
        subscription, or the desire to maintain software.
      </p>

      <p>
        There's a longer-term social hope behind all this. I imagine more ways for people to
        contribute to things that excite them and respond to needs they see around them. Much of
        that energy has to fit around the job that pays the rent. I'd like to experiment with
        arrangements that give people more room to choose how they work together and how they
        support one another.
      </p>

      <p>
        Software alone won't settle how that work gets paid for. But communities need tools for
        trying different arrangements, and they should be able to change those tools as they learn.
        Having to turn every idea into a startup is a large constraint on what we get to try.
      </p>

      <p>
        The first place I want to explore this is with my own friends. We ought to be able to make
        something useful for our shared lives, keep it small if that suits us, and let another group
        take it in a different direction. The history should help them understand what we tried. Our
        data and the tools we made should still be available if the original author moves on or an
        agent service changes.
      </p>

      <p>
        The work on <a href="https://www.inkandswitch.com/essay/local-first/"
          >local-first software</a
        > puts lasting access alongside collaboration and control. Living up to that takes ongoing care:
        backups, recovery, readable formats, and people able to maintain what we depend on. Community
        ownership needs practical ways for new people to take on that care.
      </p>

      <p>
        This remains an ambition for xNet. Even as its maker, I've found it too complicated to reach
        for in some of my own projects. A desktop prototype also leaves out people who mainly use
        their phones. Those are reasons to keep testing the idea against ordinary use, with the
        people I hope it will serve.
      </p>

      <p>
        I keep coming back to what I liked about coworking: I could spend the day inside something I
        was helping to build. There were other people there, with needs and ideas of their own. The
        place could become different because we were in it.
      </p>

      <p>
        I'd like our software to have more of that quality. A living system changes through its
        relationships with the people around it. For software, that could start quite simply: a
        community notices a need, makes something together, and keeps shaping it as their life
        changes.
      </p>

      <p>
        That's the GitHub for everyone I still want to find my way towards. We could show what we're
        working on and welcome each other's contributions, knowing that the things we make will
        remain ours to change as our lives change with them.
      </p>
    </article>

    <aside aria-label="Related reading" class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-3xl px-6">
      <div class="border-t border-gray-200 pt-8 dark:border-gray-700">
        <h2 class="text-sm font-medium">More on the foundation</h2>
        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-x-6 gap-y-3 text-sm">
          <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="/blog/data-should-work-like-soil"
            >Data Should Work Like Soil</a
          >
          <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="/blog/clutch-power">Clutch Power</a>
          <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="/blog/the-workshop-and-the-walled-garden"
            >The Workshop and the Walled Garden</a
          >
        </div>
      </div>
    </aside>
    <SeriesNav slug={post.slug} />
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>

<style>
  .office-line {
    border-inline-start: 2px solid currentColor;
    padding-inline-start: 1.25rem;
    margin-block: 2.5rem;
    font-size: clamp(1.35rem, 3vw, 1.75rem);
    line-height: 1.55;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
</style>
